1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,526
Same amino-chain, same peptide.

2
00:00:02,526 --> 00:00:03,619
Different rules.

3
00:00:03,619 --> 00:00:07,070
You might expect one peptide to have one shelf life.

4
00:00:07,070 --> 00:00:08,098
It does not.

5
00:00:08,098 --> 00:00:12,656
The published rules belong to the exact product, not just the molecule.

6
00:00:12,656 --> 00:00:14,466
Look at three real labels.

7
00:00:14,466 --> 00:00:19,375
Product A says use immediately after mixing, and throw away what is left.

8
00:00:19,375 --> 00:00:23,606
Product B says throw the pen away twenty-eight days after first use.

9
00:00:23,606 --> 00:00:26,992
Product C says fifty-six days after first use.

10
00:00:26,992 --> 00:00:30,677
Different products, different formulations, different rules.

11
00:00:30,677 --> 00:00:37,057
It can even happen with the very same active molecule: two products can carry different storage times.

12
00:00:37,057 --> 00:00:42,734
The formulation decides: the water, the preservative, the container, the pH.

13
00:00:42,734 --> 00:00:46,966
So a number you hear about the amino-chain is not a rule for your vial.

14
00:00:46,966 --> 00:00:49,921
The published label for the exact product wins.

15
00:00:49,921 --> 00:00:51,171
Day nine of ten.

16
00:00:51,171 --> 00:00:53,190
Follow so you don't miss day ten.

